<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!-- The colour of a selected row in a TableView: the app's cyan (#04C8FF), at the same alphas
       that other selected items use. See the ListView brushes in AddMusicDialog and
       ReleaseLookupDialog, which are #2404C8FF, #3004C8FF and #1404C8FF. There is one selected
       colour across the app.

       These styles must be merged into each grid's own Resources, not declared app-wide in
       App.xaml. On the WinUI head a row is drawn by a ListViewItemPresenter whose SelectedBackground
       is {ThemeResource TableViewRowBackgroundSelected} (WinUI.TableView/Themes/TableViewRow.xaml).
       That template's dictionary merges the library's own Themes/Resources.xaml, which declares this
       key. The lookup is answered there and never reaches the app's resources. The library's value
       is SubtleFillColorSecondaryBrush, which in the dark theme is #0FFFFFFF (white at 6%). Plain
       keys and ThemeDictionaries in App.xaml do not change it.

       A dictionary on the grid is reached. The row is the grid's own item container, so the lookup
       walks up through it before it leaves the tree. AddMusicDialog's ListViewItemBackgroundSelected
       is in the same place, and so are the queue grid's checkbox brushes. Both work.

       One file rather than a copy per grid, so the library's data table and the import queue look
       the same. -->

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewRowBackgroundSelected" Color="#2404C8FF"/>
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewRowBackgroundSelectedPointerOver" Color="#3004C8FF"/>
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewRowBackgroundSelectedPressed" Color="#3004C8FF"/>
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewRowBackgroundSelectedDisabled" Color="#1404C8FF"/>

  <!-- The left selection indicator bar: the same cyan at full strength, so it matches the wash. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewRowSelectionIndicatorBrush" Color="#04C8FF"/>
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewRowSelectionIndicatorPointerOverBrush" Color="#04C8FF"/>
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewRowSelectionIndicatorPressedBrush" Color="#04C8FF"/>
</ResourceDictionary>
